  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the resistance track in a potentiometer?",
      "a": "It is a curved strip of carbon or another resistive material. The longer the stretch of track the current must pass through, the more resistance it meets.",
      "asks": [
        "What is inside a volume knob?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the wiper in a potentiometer do?",
      "a": "The wiper is a springy metal contact that slides along the track as you turn the knob. Where it touches decides how much of the track is in the circuit, or what share of the voltage is picked off.",
      "asks": [
        "What moves inside a potentiometer?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does turning the knob change the volume?",
      "a": "Turning the knob moves the wiper, changing the share of the signal passed on. More signal reaching the amplifier means louder sound.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a volume control work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a dimmer switch change brightness?",
      "a": "Old dimmers used a big variable resistor, which wasted energy as heat. Modern dimmers use electronics to switch the power on and off very quickly, so the lamp gets power for only part of each cycle.",
      "asks": [
        "Do dimmers waste electricity?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a potentiometer?",
      "a": "A potentiometer, or pot, is a resistor with a third, movable contact. Used with all three legs, it splits a voltage in any ratio you choose, which is how most volume knobs work.",
      "asks": [
        "What does pot mean in electronics?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Audio volume knobs often use a logarithmic track, to match the way our ears judge loudness.",
    "A potentiometer has three terminals: the two ends of the track and the sliding wiper."
  ],
